{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["And the meaning of <b>and his uncleanness</b> [is]: that he is unclean of himself, like a zav and a metzora, also on account of a nocturnal emission, for thus it is written, for it says \"a happening\"; or that he be unclean on account of another, like the uncleanness of a human, dead or alive, and that is a zav and a metzora; or [through] an unclean beast that is not eaten, and an unclean detestable thing among birds and creeping things. \\n\\nOnce a certain Sadducee came to me and asked whether the tail-fat [ailyah] is forbidden by Torah, and I answered and said: It is true that the ailyah is called chelev, for thus it is written: \"its chelev, the ailyah,\" only our ancients permitted it and forbade all [other] chelev. Then he answered: Is not all chelev forbidden by Torah, for thus it is written: \"all chelev and all blood you shall not eat\"? And there it is written beforehand: \"a statute forever throughout your generations.\" \\n\\nAnd I also answered him: This verse is joined with the sacrifice of the shelamim, and the phrase \"a statute forever throughout your generations in all your dwellings\" is not a complete proof, for behold it is written: \"And bread and parched grain and fresh ears you shall not eat until this very day, until you bring the offering of your Elohim,\" and there too it is written \"a statute forever\" — if so, one may not eat bread in exile, for we have not brought the omer offering. \\n\\n He also countered: \"all the chelev of an ox and sheep and goat\" — \"you shall not eat.\" And I also replied that this verse too is joined with the sacrifice of the shelamim, and the proof is: \"For whoever eats chelev of the beast from which they offer\" — to exclude the chelev of all flesh that is not brought for shelamim. And the general principle is: ordinary [non-sacred] meat. Therefore in this section it says: \"and the chelev of a carcass and the chelev of a torn animal may be used for any work,\" but \"you shall not eat it.\" And it is known that the meat of a carcass and of a torn animal is forbidden, and what is forbidden is the meat; and because the chelev is not brought near the altar, perhaps a person might think it is permitted, therefore it warned: \"you shall not eat it.\" And because of this it did not mention blood. And this section comes to explain the punishment of one who eats chelev — [in the case of] sacred meat. Likewise, \"all blood\" — it added [even] to birds; therefore the chelev of birds is permitted. \\n\\nAnd the complete proof is what it says in the book Devarim concerning \"meat of desire\" [basar ta'avah], which is ordinary [non-sacred], that one may eat it all, and it excluded only its blood alone, in three places, with no mention of chelev at all. \\n\\nThen the Sadducee opened his eyes and parted his lips with an oath that he would no longer rely on his own intellect in interpreting the commandments, but would lean on the transmitted interpretations."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The parenthetical glosses in [brackets] mark places where I inserted minimal connective phrasing to preserve grammatical coherence; Ibn Ezra's argument is left as compressed as in the original."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:09:08.096054391Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}